          <td>Manual (paper or basic apps)</td>
          <td class="winner">Instant per-move validation + hint system</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Best for</td>
          <td>Kids learning arithmetic, variety seekers</td>
          <td class="winner">Pure logic fans who want the refined classic</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>If you are new to the genre, start with cross math addition puzzles to learn the rhythm. If you are hooked and want the most polished, most deductively pure version of the genre, Kakuro is the destination — and it happens to be <a href="/kakuro/">free to play online at GridPaw</a>.</p>

    <h2>Printable Cross Math Tips</h2>
    <p>Whether you are solving on paper or building worksheets, keep these reference notes handy:</p>
    <div class="print-box">
      <h3>The Most Useful Number Combinations (No Repeats)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Two cells, sum 3:</strong> 1+2 only.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cells, sum 4:</strong> 1+3 only.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cells, sum 5:</strong> 1+4, 2+3.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cells, sum 6:</strong> 1+5, 2+4.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cells, sum 17:</strong> 8+9 only.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cells, sum 16:</strong> 7+9 only.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cells, product 6:</strong> 1×6, 2×3.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cells, product 12:</strong> 2×6, 3×4.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cells, product 24:</strong> 3×8, 4×6.</li>
        <li><strong>Three cells, sum 6:</strong> 1+2+3 only.</li>
        <li><strong>Three cells, sum 7:</strong> 1+2+4 only.</li>
        <li><strong>Three cells, sum 24:</strong> 7+8+9 only.</li>
        <li><strong>Three cells, product 36:</strong> 1×4×9, 1×6×6, 2×2×9, 2×3×6, 3×3×4.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p>Print this section and keep it beside your puzzle book. The recall of these small combinations is the entire game at beginner and intermediate level.</p>

